WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.220
At the heart of our plan is a tax cut for
everyday working Americans.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.220
Donald Trump has declared "with tax reform,
we can make it morning in America again"

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.980
and that "revising our tax code is not just
a policy discussion —

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.540
it's a moral one, because we are not talking
about the government's money –

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.359
we are talking about your money, your hard
work."

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.540
So we overhaul and streamline the Internal
Revenue Service.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.779
The Republican tax plan, which would cut rates
for individuals and small businesses,

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:33.160
sounds like good policy, but it’s not.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.940
The hallmark of a good tax code is that it
doesn’t attempt social engineering

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.719
via revenue collection..

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.160
It's our money and the government shouldn't
be telling us how to spend it

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.080
or what to spend it on.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.600
And yet our tax code is larded up with all
sorts of incentives

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.320
for certain types of purchases—such as the
mortgage-interest deduction,

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.500
which is defended on the grounds that owning
a home is

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.200
morally and culturally superior to renting.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.359
It’s not by the way, and the result is market
distortions

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.900
that saddle families that would be better
off renting with mortgage debt.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.660
Trump's tax plan keeps the mortgage interest
deduction--

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.900
and the one for charitable giving, which is
another example of social engineering.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.860
Another distortion in the tax code

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.200
is that individuals can deduct the cost of
their state and local taxes

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.740
from their federal bill,

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.200
effectively allowing jurisdictions like New
York and California

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.719
to get away with charging their residents
more than they would otherwise.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.480
To its credit, the Trump plan at least attempts
to do away with this practice,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.020
although it’s doubtful this idea will survive
the legislative process..

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.610
The most important principle for tax reform

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.979
is that revenue should cover the actual costs
of government

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:39.270
so that citizens can actually make an informed
decisions

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.320
about what services they're willing to pay
for.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.560
On this score, Trump's plan is sadly business
as usual.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.869
You have not answered, though, a simple question

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:52.370
did you use that 916 million dollar loss to
avoid paying personal federal income taxes?

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:57.700
Of course I do, of course I do, and so do
all of her donors or most of her donors.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.940
First, it would take even more people off
the tax rolls.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:05.649
There are already over 40 million households
that pay no federal income tax at all

00:02:05.649 --> 00:02:10.010
and the president brags that his plan would
add another 31 million to that total.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.940
How could it be that nearly half the country
pays no taxes?

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:17.710
According to the Tax Policy Center 46.4% of
households pay no federal income tax.

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:18.900
Who are these non-payers?

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.240
As Chris Edwards of the Cato Institute writes,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.500
"taking more people off the tax rolls is not
a good way to keep the government limited.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.920
If something is 'free,' people will demand
more of it."

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.030
And the problem is much bigger than that.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:33.640
For decades now, the feds have been spending
far more in any year

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.760
than they take in via taxes.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.660
Last year, for instance, the government spent
20 percent more than it took in

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:45.240
and between 2009 and 2013, it spent 33 percent
more than it brought in.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.080
Hence annual deficits and ballooning national
debt.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.360
This is like government by Groupon: Every
year, we're getting such a great deal,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.560
of course we want more and more stuff.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.000
We'd be stupid not to.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.380
That is why the foundation of our job creation
agenda

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.360
is to fundamentally reform our tax code

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.680
for the first time in more than 30 years.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.440
Now where does Trump's plan land on this topic?

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:10.480
Who knows?

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.660
Every tax reform promises to be either be
revenue neutral

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.460
or to increase the government's haul.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:18.760
Would you be willing to raise your right hand
and look at the American people and say

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:23.460
'I Steven Mnuchin promise you that this will
be revenue neutral and deficit neutral.'

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.780
Well it will have to be revenue neutral, we
have a 21 trillion dollar debt.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.320
He says it's revenue neutral, that's Washington
speak for 'I don't know.'

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.880
In many cases, neither outcome is close to
being truthful.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.540
More to the point, after years of accumulating
debt

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.960
we need to focus on government spending first
and foremost.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.840
In 2016, the feds took in about $3 trillion
in taxes.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.000
That should be the absolute spending limit—

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.260
instead of the nearly $4 trillion Congress is talking about.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.220
Taxes aren't the price we pay for civilization—

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.140
they're the price we pay for government.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.660
And until we bind government and taxes together,

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.340
we'll be spending more and more money that
we don't have

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.980
on things we almost certainly wouldn't want
if we had to pay full price for them.

